beta

# Which update channel a tagged release from THIS branch publishes to.
# Read by scripts/release-channel.sh, which drives .github/workflows/release.yml.
# Valid values: stable, beta, dev
#
#   stable -> stable          full GitHub release, triggers the docs deploy
#   beta   -> beta + dev      GitHub prerelease; the devel track
#   dev    -> dev             GitHub prerelease; bleeding edge only
#
# This is declared per-branch rather than derived from the tag string because
# helix::version::Version discards prerelease suffixes (include/version.h), so
# tags like v1.1.0-dev1 and v1.1.0-dev2 compare EQUAL and the in-app updater
# stops offering devel builds after the first install. Declaring the channel
# here lets the devel track use plain monotonic versions (1.1.0, 1.1.1, ...)
# without those tags landing on the stable channel.
#
# This branch is the trunk and says `beta`, so a tag here reaches the beta and
# dev channels and never stable. The stable channel belongs to release/1.0,
# which carries `stable` and is the only branch a stable-fleet update may be
# tagged from.
#
# A trunk that said `stable` would publish the whole 1.1 line to every stable
# install on the next tag, and release.yml's pre-upload downgrade guard cannot
# refuse it: 1.1.0 > 1.0.0 is a forward move. That is why this value and the
# release/1.0 cut belong to one change rather than two.
# See docs/devel/RELEASE_1_0_CHECKLIST.md § "The atomic branch cut".
